@@ -24,11 +24,17 @@ SQLite WAL capture reconstructs the last complete committed state in memory. It

 The capture accepts checkpointed WAL headers without peers and with an empty WAL. It refuses rollback journals, orphan WAL or SHM files, and peers beside a database outside WAL mode. It pairs peers only with the exact database filename under the same held root. Channel names can contain letters, digits, dots, underscores, and hyphens.

-The SHM file is a transient index. The capture reads and rechecks its bytes but derives no database content from it. A change to SHM, WAL, the main file, or the selected manifest causes refusal. Invalid checksums, torn frames, and old-generation tails also cause refusal. This includes reused WAL tails that SQLite itself could recover. The capture returns no partial report and makes no automatic retry.
+The SHM file is a transient index. The capture reads and rechecks its bytes but derives no database content from it. A present SHM file must have two identical initialized headers, valid native-endian checksums, and matching page size, salts, and checksum order. Its committed frame, database size, and last frame checksum must match the validated WAL. A later current-generation commit or invalid current-generation frame causes refusal.
+
+The capture accepts old-generation tails only after a validated current-generation commit matches the SHM header. It excludes frames with different salt-1 values and refuses any current-generation frame after an old frame. The overwritten prefix prevents independent checksum validation of obsolete payload bytes. Their exclusion depends on the matching committed frontier. This is not proof that obsolete bytes are intact.
+
+Missing SHM permits only complete WAL validation without old-generation tails. Malformed, stale, or conflicting SHM headers cause refusal. A reused WAL without a current-generation commit also causes refusal. Empty WAL files need no SHM, but present SHM headers must describe the main image with zero committed frames. The capture does not use SHM page maps or locks to reconstruct pages.
+
+A change to SHM, WAL, the main file, or the selected manifest causes refusal. Invalid current-generation checksums and torn frames also cause refusal. The capture returns no partial report and makes no automatic retry.

 Each WAL and SHM file shares the existing file count and source-byte limits. Each file is at most 64 MiB. Each reconstructed image is at most 64 MiB, with an additional page-offset index of at most 512 KiB. The capture checks those bounds before it allocates the image or index. It processes one image at a time and scans the WAL at most twice. The SQLite VFS, connection lifetime, and error redaction remain unchanged.

-The format and checksum rules come from [the SQLite file format](https://sqlite.org/fileformat.html#walformat). [SQLite's recovery rules](https://sqlite.org/walformat.html#recovery) describe the transient SHM index.
+The format and checksum rules come from [the SQLite file format](https://sqlite.org/fileformat.html#walformat). [SQLite's WAL-index format](https://sqlite.org/walformat.html#walidxfmt) defines its duplicated headers. [SQLite's recovery rules](https://sqlite.org/walformat.html#recovery) describe the transient index.

 SQLite errors use one fixed message without paths, identifiers, or source text. The VFS enforces these restrictions even when the pinned SQLite build omits safety PRAGMAs.

